  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who Polynesian Village is best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Anyone who wants an easy trip to Magic Kingdom.</strong> You can ride the resort monorail or take the boat. Theme Park View rooms look across the lagoon toward the park.</li>
      <li><strong>Families of five who don't want a suite.</strong> Most standard rooms sleep five, with two queen beds and a day bed, at a Deluxe resort.</li>
      <li><strong>People who care about dining at the hotel.</strong> 'Ohana, Kona Cafe, Trader Sam's and Pineapple Lanai are all on site, so you are not limited to a food court.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The nightly rate is the deciding factor.</strong> This is a Deluxe resort. In 2026, a Resort View room ran about $771 to $1,371 a night at full price, tax included.</li>
      <li><strong>Your parks are mostly EPCOT or Hollywood Studios.</strong> EPCOT takes a monorail change at the TTC. Hollywood Studios is a bus.</li>
      <li><strong>You are booking during construction.</strong> Disney has listed work on the front drive and bus area, and exterior maintenance that may block lagoon views from 'Ohana. Check whether that is still going on before you book a view. <span class="verify">[verify if still underway]</span></li>
    </ul>
    <span class="ryan">[Ryan: who you think this resort is really for, from your own stays. Two or three sentences.]</span>
  </section>

